<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-testing-out-exercises" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Testing Out Exercises</title>

  <p>
    This section is a place to try out different exercises described in chapter 3 of The PreTeXt Guide and see how they differ. The first type of exercise is a <em>divisional exercise</em>.
  </p>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Solve the equation
        <md>
          3x + 2 = 17
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Try to get <m>x</m> all by itself on one side of the equation.
      </p>
    </hint>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>x=5</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
      <p>
        To solve this equation, we first subtract <m>2</m> from both sides, giving us
        <md>
          3x = 15
        </md>
        Then, we divide both sides by <m>3</m> to cancel out the coefficient of <m>x</m>, which gives us
        <md>
          x = \frac{15}{3} = 5
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Now, we will consider the second type of exercise, which is an <em>inline exercise</em>. These are placed within a paragraph itself and mostly just contain a statement. For instance, <exercise>
      <p>
        Solve the equation
          <md>
            3x + 2 = 17
          </md>    
      </p>
    </exercise>
  The point of this section is to see how these look differently in print.
  </p>
  <p>
    A third type of exercise is the <em>reading questions</em>. These are housed in their own division as follows.
  </p>
  <reading-questions xml:id="rqs-testing-out-exercises">
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        This is a reading question?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Check the guide.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>
  </reading-questions>

</section>